/bin/
/repo-metrics

# SQLite database and its WAL sidecars
*.db
*.db-wal
*.db-shm

# A local go.work would defeat the GOWORK=off arrangement in the Makefile
go.work
go.work.sum

# Coverage artifacts, in case anyone runs the tool against this repo.
#
# Any .out rather than coverage.out specifically. This repo's own docs and CI
# hand -coverprofile several different names (cover.out, all.out, the temp files
# the cross-check recipe writes), and naming one of them meant a stray profile
# from a verification run was checked in and had to be pulled back out of a
# commit. Nothing here legitimately ends in .out.
*.out
/reports/

# What the tool leaves in this repo root when it is pointed at itself.
#
# That is the documented path rather than a hypothetical: `repo-metrics init`
# writes its config into the current directory, and the starter config's first
# repo entry points at that same directory on purpose, so a fresh checkout can
# run `collect` and `report` and see real output before editing anything.
# `report` writes to stdout, so `report.md` is wherever someone sent it. Both
# hold one operator's local setup — the repos they happen to have checked out,
# what their last run said — and neither is source.
#
# Untracked is not good enough here, because this repo is one of the repos being
# measured. Collection reads `git status --porcelain`, which counts untracked
# files, so either one left in the root sets git_dirty on every snapshot of this
# repo and earns each one the report warning that its numbers belong to no
# commit. The tool would be reporting on a mess it made itself.
#
# Anchored, because the basenames are not unique in this tree.
# `examples/repo-metrics.yaml` is the tracked polyglot config the README sends
# people to copy, and an unanchored pattern would ignore that too.
/repo-metrics.yaml
/report.md
